window.PROJECTS_DATA=[
{id:'ai-butler',highlight:'Redesigned the in-room smart speaker’s call flow and UI; 7 hotels renewed or upgraded within a month of launch',coverNatural:true,projectType:'Hardware–Software Integration · Interaction Design',timeline:'2023 – Now',platform:'Android App',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'From a single sensory experience to a scalable system.',labels:['Hardware–Software Integration','Interaction Design'],tag:'Smart Speaker',pairTag:'Voice Interaction Design',year:'Flagship Project',title:'AVA Smart Speaker Interaction System',
scope:'Responsible for UI/UX: interface, interaction and state design, plus every animation, built from scratch in After Effects',collaborators:'PM · Frontend & backend engineers · LLM engineers · Japanese sales team',
oneLiner:'An in-room AI voice butler for hotels. I designed its interface, interaction states and animations so that guests, often looking from across the room, understand it at a glance and can use it without a tutorial.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',items:['An AI voice butler that sits on the desk in each hotel room. Guests talk to it to order room service, request amenities, control the TV and lights, or start a meditation mode synced with the room’s lighting.','I worked on three things: making the AI easier to use, turning the home screen into a visual system every hotel brand can use, and designing moments that help guests relax.']},
{heading:'Results at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[{stat:'7',statUnit:'hotels in one month',title:'7 hotels renewed or upgraded within a month',text:'Within a month of the new call flow going live, 7 hotels renewed or upgraded to the new UI and call model, and newly onboarded hotels choose the latest UI.'},{stat:'+1',statUnit:'new hotel client',title:'Closed a new partnership',text:'Launching the Wellness meditation feature directly led to a partnership with that hotel client'},{stat:'+233%',statUnit:'clicks on promoted content',title:'Hotel picks finally got noticed',text:'After the home screen redesign, clicks on the content one hotel wanted to promote grew 233%, and 37.6% of guests tapped it first after waking the device.'}]},
{pin:1,type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,small:true,title:'UI refresh, now live',text:'Redesigned around information hierarchy and clearer wording. For example, alarm dates now read “Today” or “Thu, Sep 10” instead of “06/03 Mon”, matching the date formats guests from different countries are used to.',compare:[{label:'Dial pad',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-dial-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-dial-after.png'},{label:'In a call',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-call-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-call-after.webp'},{label:'Delivery service',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-delivery-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-delivery-after.png'},{label:'Clock',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-clock-before.png',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-clock-after.png'}]}]},
{heading:'Making the AI Easier to Use',type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'ai-butler-call-states',natural:true,title:'Learning by Using, Not by Tutorial',text:'Surveys and feedback from our sales team told us guests found the AI “not smart enough”, and that they didn’t know how to use an unfamiliar product. Usability tests showed the real problem: guests couldn’t tell what state the AI was in. They didn’t know when they could speak or how to wake it, and weren’t sure whether waking it had worked.\n\nHow might we help first-time guests talk to the AI, without a long tutorial?\n\nWe first considered a first-use tutorial. But it was too long, and it would stop guests from exploring other features first. So I kept it simple. A push message on the home screen offers an optional entry for guests who want to learn more. Inside the real flow, text and animation hint at the state: while listening, the screen stays still and shows “I’m listening”; animation appears only while the AI is processing. Guests learn as they use it. Within a month of launch, 7 hotels renewed or upgraded.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-phone-merge',natural:true,title:'Next: Merging with the Existing Phone Module',text:'When the conversation feature moved into the phone module, it had to merge with existing functions like calling other rooms and transferring to the front desk. I refreshed the interface with engineers and the PM, and the state system from the previous version carried over without redesign.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-phone-merge-r',natural:true},{imgId:'ai-butler-iot',title:'Channels First, Input Sources Second',text:'We wanted the device to work more deeply with in-room appliances.\n\nHow might we let guests keep their familiar remote-control habits, while using the screen to show information a remote can’t?\n\nAfter connecting with IPTV vendors, we found each vendor’s control behavior was very different. Japanese TVs also add a layer of input sources above channels. At first I thought Japanese TVs needed their own custom interface and flow. But interviews with our Japanese sales team and people familiar with life in Japan showed that channels are still how people remember what to watch. So I made channel browsing and categories the main view, helping guests find a channel easily, and moved input sources to a second-level menu at the top. I also used familiar icons wherever I could. I considered “ON / OFF” text for the power control, but translations would break the small layout in other languages. It is now used by hotels in several countries, including Japan and Singapore.'}]},
{heading:'A Home Screen System for Every Brand',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'From a Fragmented Grid to Brand-Led Imagery',text:'Usage data from several hotels showed that music always had the most taps and usage, 35–46%. But for hotels, music doesn’t bring much value. The data held another clue. The second most-tapped item was a full-image button. I suspected the grid made it look like a plain picture, and it had no call-to-action showing it could be tapped.\n\nHow might we help guests see what the hotel wants to promote first, without changing the grid system?\n\nI let the background hold an image, made button transparency adjustable, and enlarged the image button. The screen is now led by the hotel’s brand imagery instead of scattered tiles. The grid stays the same, so nothing had to be redeveloped. Clicks on promoted content grew 233% at one hotel, and 37.6% of guests tapped it first after waking the device. At another hotel, marketing clicks grew 2.9×.',compare:[{label:'Home screen',before:'slot-assets/ava-ba-home-before.webp',after:'slot-assets/ava-ba-home-after.webp'}]},{imgId:'ai-butler-home-custom',title:'Home Screen Customization',text:'One system, many brand looks: home screen colors, fonts and layout adjust per hotel, from understated luxury to fresh minimalism, with no redevelopment.'}]},
{heading:'Designing Moments That Help Guests Relax',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'In Meditation, the AI Steps Back to Guide',text:'The challenge was how to design something as abstract as relaxation. In this moment, the device becomes a supporting guide. It combines the room’s lighting, music and slow-paced animation to immerse guests in a calming atmosphere.\n\nHow might we let guests finish the whole session without continually tapping the screen?\n\nOne tap or one sentence starts the session and carries it to the end. I worked with the PM to define the lighting and music themes, and produced every animation myself in After Effects. The wake word can also detect a low or negative mood, such as “I feel upset”, and guide the guest into this flow. The feature directly led to a partnership with a new hotel.'},{imgId:'ai-butler-wellness-video',ratio:'4/3'},{pairIds:['ai-butler-festive-anim','ai-butler-festive-anim-2','ai-butler-festive-anim-3','ai-butler-festive-anim-4'],title:'Holiday-Themed Animations',text:'I also produce holiday-themed animations for marketing, adding seasonal atmosphere to the room.'}]},
{heading:'Results',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Hotel Feedback',text:'“Now housekeeping requests get a fast response, restaurant reservations are easy to arrange, and guest preferences are recorded automatically — all thanks to the leading AI technology of the Aiello Voice Assistant.” — M Social Hotel Phuket, Thailand.'}]},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['Crafting one sensory experience and systematizing everyday needs look like opposite skills, but both answer the same question: what does the user actually need right now?','Next time, I would track how often guests repeat themselves or interrupt the device after launch, to measure how much the clearer states helped.','Meditation also taught me that a good interface isn’t always a complete one. Sometimes the UI should step back and let animation, sound and light lead.']}
]},
{id:'aca-ai',highlight:'Redesigned the AI conversation flow for hotel phone orders; orders completed by AI rose from 0% to 95%',coverRatio:'2/1',projectType:'UX Design · Data Analysis',timeline:'2026 – Now',platform:'Phone',timelineFirst:true,labels:['Voice Interaction Design','Conversation Flow Design'],tag:'AI Phone Agent',pairTag:'Conversation Flow Design',tag3:'Test Scenario Planning',year:'Flow Optimization',title:'AI Voice Agent for Hotel In-Room Dining',
scope:'Data analysis, requirements translation, VUI flow planning',collaborators:'PM · Backend engineers',
oneLiner:'I analyzed thousands of real phone calls, then broke down and rebuilt human agents’ ordering logic into service guidelines an AI can execute reliably — so every call sounds like it’s being handled by a seasoned agent.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Results at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,stat:'95%',statUnit:'orders completed by AI',title:'0% → 95% of phone orders completed by AI',text:'Room service is 16% of all calls, and the AI used to transfer every order to a human. Now, after launch, it completes them on its own.',noImage:true},
{compact:true,title:'Smoother conversations, faster delivery',text:'The AI asks fewer unnecessary questions, and engineers implement directly from the guidelines, shortening regression testing',noImage:true}
]},
{pin:1,type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,small:true,title:'Scenarios the AI agent now handles',text:'Five situations from real calls. Switch between them to compare the AI’s replies before and after the guidelines.',chat:{scenarios:[{label:'Menu questions',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Do you have Hainanese chicken rice on the menu?'},{who:'ai',text:'For menu information I need to transfer you to a staff member. Shall I transfer you?'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Do you have Hainanese chicken rice on the menu?'},{who:'ai',text:'Yes, we do! Would you like me to order one for you?'}]},{label:'Lookup failures',before:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like to add a side of fries.'},{who:'ai',text:'Tool call failed.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like to add a side of fries.'},{who:'ai',text:'Sorry, I can’t find fries right now. Would you like to see our other snacks?'}]},{label:'Describing dishes',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Any dessert you’d recommend?'},{who:'ai',text:'I found tiramisu and brownies in the menu.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Any dessert you’d recommend?'},{who:'ai',text:'Our tiramisu is a favorite, and we also have a warm brownie. Which would you like to try?'}]},{label:'No back-to-back questions',before:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like a steak.'},{who:'ai',text:'How would you like it cooked? Which sauce? What’s your room number?'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'I’d like a steak.'},{who:'ai',text:'Sure, one steak. How would you like it cooked?'}]},{label:'Special requests',before:[{who:'guest',text:'Can I get the pasta without garlic?'},{who:'ai',text:'I can’t answer that. Let me transfer you to a staff member.'}],after:[{who:'guest',text:'Can I get the pasta without garlic?'},{who:'ai',text:'I’ll note “no garlic” on your order. Or would you like to check with the kitchen directly?'}]}]}}]},
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'Phone ordering was the feature hotels cared about most — yet it still hadn’t been built',sideChart:[
{label:'Human agent request',pct:100,value:'27%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'In-room dining',pct:59,value:'16%',color:'#85B7EB',bold:true},
{label:'Laundry',pct:41,value:'11%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Other',pct:41,value:'11%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Amenities',pct:33,value:'9%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Room Reservation',pct:33,value:'9%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Technical Repair',pct:19,value:'5%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Other Topic',pct:15,value:'4%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Hotel Policy',pct:11,value:'3%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'F&B Facility',pct:7,value:'2%',color:'#B0B0B4'},
{label:'Transport&Travel',pct:4,value:'1%',color:'#B0B0B4'}
],items:['This is an AI voice agent built for the hospitality industry, designed to solve a structural problem: hotels have limited staff, yet must respond to guest requests 24 hours a day.','Call data showed phone ordering was the most requested service, ahead of laundry, amenities and maintenance, and it drives F&B revenue. But menus change by time of day, stock changes in real time and guests make custom requests, so ordering was left out of the first launch.','I led the conversation flow design, breaking down how human agents handle phone orders into a flow an AI can follow.']},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Too many scenarios: ordering had to become a flowchart developers could build.','Unclear limits: I had to know what the AI could handle before agreeing on scope with hotels.','Hard to verify: checking whether the AI followed the rules meant digging through system logs.','Concise vs. explicit: the AI needs clear rules, but longer guidelines confuse it.','Too much data: I needed a fast way to find the records that matter.']},
{heading:'Process',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'From a UI flowchart to a main flow and edge cases',text:'I started the way I would for a screen: a step-by-step flowchart. But voice conversations don’t follow a fixed order. Guests skip questions or ask things out of sequence, so the chart kept growing, and the team struggled to discuss it or agree on where to start.\n\nSo I changed my approach. With a data analyst, I used AI to analyze thousands of real calls and shared the insights with the team. Then I split ordering into one main flow and a set of edge cases.\n\nIn the next meeting, the team could finally see what was still unsolved, decide which cases the AI handles and which go to a human, and agree on a strategy for hotel requests.',imgId:'aca-flow-1',natural:true},
{title:'Building team consensus, then aligning with hotels',text:'After the team agreed on the AI’s boundaries, we confirmed service principles with hotels. I turned them into a Policy document the AI agent reads, plus test scenarios to validate the flow.',imgId:'aca-flow-2',natural:true},
{title:'Applying VUI design principles to reduce cognitive load',text:'I followed NN/g’s voice interface (VUI) guidelines: voice has no screen to rescan, so information must come in small chunks. The AI gives only what the next step needs and confirms before going deeper. This became a baseline rule for every ordering flow.',imgId:'aca-flow-3',noImage:true},
{text:'I also wrote rules for moments when the AI didn’t sound like a professional agent:',noImage:true},
{title:'• Don’t read out system terms when a lookup fails',text:'The AI used to read out system error messages. Now it uses plain, reassuring language.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Describe dishes in a professional, hospitality-appropriate tone',text:'The AI used to open with system-like phrases such as “I see…”. Now it leads with the dish itself, like a hotel agent would.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Don’t ask follow-ups back-to-back when a guest skips a question',text:'The AI used to chase every skipped detail right away, which felt pushy. A confirmation checkpoint at the end now collects anything missing.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Don’t transfer to a human just because an answer can’t be found',text:'The AI used to transfer to a human whenever an answer wasn’t on the menu, adding work for staff. Now guests choose: add a note to the order and continue, or talk to a person.',noImage:true},
{text:'These weren’t one-off fixes. Every new issue goes through the same method:',noImage:true},
{title:'Turning discovered issues into guidelines',noImage:true},
{title:'• Categorize the issue first, then identify the root cause',text:'Is it the guideline, the data or the flow? A clear cause makes each change easy to explain to the team.',imgId:'aca-flow-4'},
{title:'• Write guidelines as Do / Don’t',text:'Explicit behavior rules leave less room for the AI to guess, and engineers can implement them directly.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Restructure the RAG knowledge base the AI queries',text:'Consolidate the scattered information hotels provide into a consistent structure, so the AI retrieves more accurately and answers more reliably.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Build a Claude Skill to speed up validation',text:'Tracing what an AI reply was based on used to take manual digging. I built a Skill that connects to Grafana and lists the MCP tools and knowledge base data used in a call, cutting validation to seconds.',imgId:'aca-flow-6',imgId2:'aca-flow-6b',natural:true}
]},
{heading:'Results & Impact',type:'text',items:['Conversations became smoother with fewer unnecessary questions, making calls easier for guests. Clear guidelines also made implementation easier and shortened regression testing.']},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['Voice design has to start from real data. Many problems only show up when you read through real conversations, not through reasoning alone.']}
]},
{id:'guestweb',highlight:'One system that lets every hotel launch a branded guest website; one hotel reached about NT$270K in monthly F&B revenue after launch',projectType:'UX Research · Design System · Web UI Design',platform:'Web (guest-facing in-room interface + hotel admin back office)',timeline:'2024 – Now',coverHeadline:'One system, endless styles',labels:['UX Research','Design System','Web UI Design'],tag:'Multi-brand Website',pairTag:'Content Management System',year:'Systems Thinking',title:'GuestWeb Hotel Digital Concierge',
scope:'Responsible for UI/UX: competitive research, design system, and guest web and back-office interface design',collaborators:'PM · Frontend & backend engineers · Hotel brand contacts',
oneLiner:'A hotel service web page guests open by scanning a QR code. I designed its design system, guest pages and back office, so every hotel can launch a page in its own brand style without engineers.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'This system lets every hotel quickly launch a service website that feels “built for their brand” — while all of them run on one shared, maintained architecture',items:['Guests scan an in-room QR code to open the hotel’s own front desk page. From pre-arrival to check-out, they can look up information, order food, request deliveries, book rides and arrange laundry, without downloading an app. Hotels manage it all through a SaaS back office.','Every hotel wants a page that fits its brand and can go live without engineers. So I designed flexible templates and components, with a back office simple enough for hotels to customize themselves.','I worked on three things: a design system that restyles the whole interface from one brand color, a system that serves very different types of hotels, and a back office that hotel staff without a design background can use with ease.']},
{heading:'Results at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,stat:'NT$270K',statUnit:'monthly F&B revenue, one hotel',title:'Revenue growth',noImage:true},
{compact:true,stat:'384',statUnit:'calls saved in a month',title:'Staff time saved',noImage:true},
{compact:true,title:'Development cost efficiency',text:'New brands launch without building a new system; the shared architecture greatly reduces maintenance and scaling costs',noImage:true}
]},
{pin:1,type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,small:true,title:'Back office and guest page, live',text:'The final design: a back office hotels can edit freely and understand at a glance, with the guest page updating to match.',media:'slot-assets/guestweb-builder.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/guestweb-builder-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1400/840'}]},
{heading:'Early Research & Design System',type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'guestweb-3-1',ratio:'1.9/1',title:'One Brand Color Restyles the Whole Interface',text:'A hotel’s web page is part of its brand image, and guests use it before, during and after their stay. There are many competing content-editing products, so we needed to find what would make ours stand out. I studied competitors such as Duve, Guestnet, myma.ai and hotelfriend, and found that all of them offer a high degree of customization.\n\nHow might we meet different hotels’ needs with one architecture, while letting hotels deploy and launch on their own, quickly?\n\nI built a tokenized design system, and agreed with the frontend engineers on which components hotels can change and which system colors must stay fixed. A hotel only needs to provide one brand color; it expands into a full color scale and restyles the whole interface at once. After launch, Holiday Inn reached about NT$270K in monthly F&B revenue.'}]},
{heading:'A Multi-Brand Design System',type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'guestweb-case-issue',ratio:'1/1',title:'Component Styles, Not Fixed Templates',text:'Our first MVP had a single default interface with a middle-ground text-to-image ratio, because we didn’t yet know what hotels would need. As we added clients, we found that different types of hotels imagined their pages very differently. Luxury hotels wanted plenty of images to set the mood, while business hotels wanted clear information; Japanese hotels, for example, wanted complete text to reassure guests.\n\nHow might we build one system that is flexible, yet easy for hotel staff to maintain?\n\nOnce this need came in, my first proposal was to split the scope into a few fixed templates. But I soon found they weren’t flexible enough to fit every hotel. So I started from the most basic level, the component styles: the same component can switch between different text-and-image styles, and hotel staff simply pick a style in the back office. We went on to sign hotels of every type, from luxury to business.'},{imgId:'guestweb-case-impl',ratio:'4/3'},{imgId:'guestweb-3-1-r',natural:true}]},
{heading:'The Back-Office Editor',type:'text-image',items:[{pairIds:['guestweb-3-0b','guestweb-3-0c'],title:'Designing Around Each Task’s Mental Model',text:'We wanted hotel staff to edit their home page themselves, so the marketing team could update campaigns and seasonal offers, a need they raised in interviews. Competitors’ back offices varied widely in how much freedom they gave; some even had users build pages from a blank canvas. Interviews also showed that the people managing this content are often in marketing or IT, usually on small teams.\n\nHow might we help people with no design background quickly build a good-looking web page?\n\nI used AI to quickly build a mock website for interviews and internal usability tests, and found that different tasks follow different mental models. Page editing goes from large to small: people want to see the whole page first, then drill into details. Menu management goes from small to large: staff build a product library first, then add items to menus one by one. So I gave these two back offices different structures and workflows. Hotel staff using them gave positive feedback.'},{imgId:'guestweb-3-0d',natural:true,caption:'Using an AI-built mock website to test users’ mental models when operating the back-office system'},{imgId:'guestweb-mental-model',ratio:'4/3'},{noImage:true,title:'Giving Up Free Layout for a Grid',text:'Hotels wanted to adjust their home page layout themselves. I first wanted Webflow-style free layout, but engineers pointed out that calculating widths on the fly would be costly to maintain.\n\nHow might we let hotels edit their home page freely, without letting engineering maintenance costs spiral?\n\nFree layout is Webflow’s selling point and worth heavy investment. Our product has only a few page types, so trading a little flexibility for lower maintenance made more sense. I switched to a grid with a few fixed sizes, and the back office shows how much space is left. Hotels found it flexible enough, engineering agreed, and it shipped.'}]},
{heading:'Results & Impact',type:'text-image',items:[
{noImage:true,title:'Results by Hotel',text:'Holiday Inn: about NT$270K in monthly F&B revenue and 450 orders after launch.\niHotel: about NT$160K in monthly revenue and 490 orders.\nHotel Clover saved 384 phone calls in one month, while receiving 204 proactive guest inquiries.'},
{noImage:true,title:'Hotel Feedback',text:'“This channel lets us engage with guests in a better way — not only keeping them up to date on the latest offers in real time, but also ensuring they never miss any information, for a smoother, more engaging stay.” — Holiday Inn & Suites Saigon Airport.\n“Since we started working together, our F&B revenue has grown 30× compared to the same period, and the Aiello Voice Assistant is the best low-friction ordering tool on the market today.” — iHotel.'}
]},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['Custom home pages and menus also have to match each hotel’s ordering system data. Design isn’t only about how screens look; the data underneath has to line up.']},
{type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'guestweb-3-1b'}]},
]},
{id:'tms-ds',coverNatural:true,timeline:'2024 – Now',highlight:'Built a design system for a back office that had none, then turned it into a one-week proposal prototype that helped win a new client',projectType:'Design System · AI Workflow · Sales Enablement',platform:'Web back office',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'A scalable, easy-to-maintain admin back-office component library shared by design and engineering',labels:['Design System','AI Workflow','Sales Enablement'],title:'TMS Back-Office Design System',
scope:'Design system lead · AI-built component library · Proposal prototype',collaborators:'PM · Frontend engineers · Sales',
oneLiner:'I rebuilt the back office’s scattered components into a design system defined by real usage, based on the live product rather than old mockups, then used AI to turn it into a working code library. It let us deliver a clickable proposal in one week and win a major Japanese hotel group.',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'A back office built fast to launch, without a real design system',items:['TMS is the back office hotels use to manage tasks and services, and the same platform behind GuestWeb’s admin tools. The first version was built quickly to validate the product, so it never had a complete design system.','I built on the existing design: audited what had actually shipped, defined every component by how it is used, and turned the result into a code library the whole team could build from.']},
{heading:'Results at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,stat:'1 wk',statUnit:'from idea to working prototype',title:'Sales enablement: won a major Japanese hotel group',text:'A clickable prototype built from the component library helped sales close a hotel chain under a Japanese listed group.',noImage:true},
{compact:true,stat:'4 → 1',statUnit:'styles per component',title:'One spec per component',text:'Components that had 3–4 different styles now follow a single spec.',noImage:true},
{compact:true,title:'From live site to code',text:'The design system ships as a clickable HTML component library, plus Markdown brand guidelines engineers follow when generating pages.',noImage:true}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Inconsistent components: built quickly to validate the product, the first version had 3–4 styles for the same component.','Mockups didn’t match the live site: the components in the design files differed a lot from what had actually shipped.','Digging through old files: designers had to search many old mockups to decide when to use a component and which style was right.','A one-week proposal: a major Japanese hotel group needed a proposal within a week, with no time to draw static mockups from scratch.']},
{heading:'Process',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'Start from what actually shipped',text:'Mockups alone would miss styles that only existed in production. I had AI write a crawler that recorded the component styles on the live site. Once Claude gained Playwright, I had it open the real pages and inspect them directly.'},{noImage:true,title:'Bring the live site back into Figma',text:'I built a plugin that turns live web components into Figma files, so the design files finally matched what users actually see.'}]},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'A full inventory',text:'The audit covered everything from basic components to dialogs, dropdowns and date and location pickers, up to the layout rules for whole pages.'},{noImage:true,title:'Scenario first, then style',text:'For each component I defined which situation it belongs to, how users behave there, and which style follows. Designers no longer have to dig through old files for the answer, and the 3–4 variants became one.'},{noImage:true,gallery:[{label:'Dialog',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-dialog.png'},{label:'Alert Dialog',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-alert.png'},{label:'Date Picker',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-picker.png'},{label:'List Item',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-list.png'},{label:'Table',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-table.png'},{label:'Table Types',src:'slot-assets/tms-ds-spec-table-types-v2.png'}],caption:'Each component spec lists its anatomy, its variants, and the scenarios each variant is used in.'}]},
{type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'tms-ds-flow',natural:true},{noImage:true,title:'Tokens and a clickable component library',text:'I used Figma MCP to read component values and had Claude Code convert them into design tokens, then generate the component library from the token structure as a clickable HTML page.'},{noImage:true,title:'Brand guidelines for engineers',text:'I also wrote a set of Markdown guidelines, so engineers follow the company’s brand rules when they generate early pages.'},{noImage:true,media:'slot-assets/tms-ds-component-library.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/tms-ds-component-library-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1280/688',title:'Tokenizing the Back-Office Design System into an AI Skill',text:'I turned the specs into a format AI can read, so it generates on-spec design proposals for validation instead of building each from scratch. The output goes straight into engineering discussions and usability tests, shortening the validation cycle.'}]},
{heading:'Results & Impact',type:'text-image',items:[{noImage:true,title:'The client',text:'The client was the hotel business of a Japanese listed financial group, running more than ten hotels and hot-spring ryokan across Japan with about 3,500 rooms, and a key account for the company.'},{noImage:true,title:'A working prototype in one week',text:'Within a week, I explored ideas, aligned with the team, and assembled a working web prototype from the component library.'},{noImage:true,media:'slot-assets/tms-ds-proposal-prototype.mp4',poster:'slot-assets/tms-ds-proposal-prototype-poster.webp',mediaRatio:'1400/672',caption:'The proposal prototype: clients could filter requests, open details and edit them, just like the real product.'},{noImage:true,title:'Built for clients and sales',text:'The client could try it on their own computer, and sales could present straight from it instead of static screenshots. We won the account.'}]},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['A design system has to stay in sync with what is live. When the product departs from the spec, I record the exception as a reference for the next change, rather than letting the two drift apart. It also means checking that every component behaves the same way across the product, not just that it looks the same.']}
]},
{id:'nexus',highlight:'Shipped in 2021',dark:true,coverNatural:true,coverHeadline:'Readable at a glance, built to look the part.',projectType:'UI Design · Hardware–Software Integration',timeline:'2020',platform:'Streaming controller panel + desktop software',timelineFirst:true,labels:['UI Design','Hardware–Software Integration','Motion Design'],title:'Live Streamer NEXUS',
oneLiner:'A streaming controller and its companion software. Starting from how streamers actually use it mid-broadcast, I built a visual system that makes every state readable at a glance on a small screen, without giving up the bold esports look the product needed.',
scope:'Dynamic UI components for the hardware panel and desktop software, boot animation, LED lighting scripts, icon library, UI string translation',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'Streamers glance at the controller. They don’t study it.',items:['NEXUS is AVerMedia’s streaming software, paired with the Live Streamer NEXUS controller. Streamers use its touchscreen and knobs to switch capture sources, mix audio and trigger hotkeys.','Mid-stream, attention is on the game and the audience; the controller gets only a glance. So every button has to show what it does and whether it’s on, in that split second.']},
{type:'cards',items:[{title:'Before',text:'Mid-stream, streamers often had to open several programs and adjust audio levels and aspect ratios'},{title:'With NEXUS',text:'Preset multiple streaming scenes, then swipe the controller’s screen to switch between them instantly'}]},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'2021',statUnit:'mass-produced',title:'Shipped in 2021',text:'Launched as a mass-produced product: AVerMedia Live Streamer NEXUS (model AX310).'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'State readable at a glance',text:'Hotkeys pair each app’s brand color with motion: on is full color and animated, off dims to a dark tone.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'One look across hardware and software',text:'The same components run on the controller’s touchscreen and in the desktop app, backed by an icon library with five interaction states.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'A complete visual asset system',text:'A boot animation with a millisecond-timed LED script, custom hotkey icons, and an app icon in 9 sizes from 16 to 1024 px.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['A glance, not a gaze: streamers look down for a split second mid-stream, so state has to register instantly','A small screen: on the controller’s touchscreen, subtle changes in an icon’s shape are easy to miss','Same function, different channels: once NEXUS integrated platforms like OBS and Twitch, buttons with the same function but for different channels could appear on the same page, and had to be told apart at a glance','Two displays, one component: the same UI appears on the hardware touchscreen and in the desktop software','Clarity without losing flair: this is a product for streamers and gamers, so it also has to look bold and polished']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Designing for a glance: from text labels to color and motion',text:'The original icons marked state with tiny ON / OFF text, easy to miss on a small screen. After adding OBS and Twitch, same-function buttons for different channels also had to be told apart.\n\nMy first version used color families for channels and built the state into the icon. My manager and fellow designers felt the contrast should be stronger for quick mid-stream use.\n\nIn the final version, each hotkey uses its app’s brand color: full color and animated when on, dimmed when off. The state reads even from the corner of the eye.',noImage:true,iconCols:5,iconGrid:['slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-1-mixer.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-2-share.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-3-layers.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-4-camera.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-on-5-record.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-1-mixer.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-2-layout.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-3-layers.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-4-store.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-off-5-record.png','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-1-folder.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-2-mixer.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-3-sound.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-4-seat.gif','slot-assets/nexus-hotkey-blue-5-play.gif']},
{title:'• Customizable, without breaking the system',text:'In NEXUS’s online editor, streamers can set their own hotkey backgrounds and images. I drew icon sets in several styles so personalized layouts still look polished.',noImage:true,iconCols:5,iconGrid:['slot-assets/nexus-custom-1-guitar.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-2-award.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-3-kaomoji.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-4-moon.png','slot-assets/nexus-custom-5-device.png']},
{title:'One visual language across hardware and software',text:'The desktop app uses a dark base to match the controller. I drew a clean line-icon library and specified five interaction states: Normal, Mouse over, Click, Disable and Focus.',imgId:'nexus-icon-library',natural:true},
{title:'Visual flair that carries the brand: boot animation and lighting',text:'Clarity sets the rules, but the product should also excite at power-on. With a flashy esports concept, I animated the AVerMedia logo for the boot screen and designed LED effects that flash in sync.',imgId:'nexus-boot',natural:true},
{title:'• Lighting scripts timed to the millisecond',text:'The lighting changes fast, so I scripted each pattern precisely, millisecond by millisecond.',imgId:'nexus-light-script',natural:true},
{title:'• App icon',text:'Based on the controller’s knobs and the software’s signature orange, I drew the app icon in 9 sizes, from 16 to 1024 px.',imgId:'nexus-app-icon',natural:true}
]},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'nexus-final',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['NEXUS taught me to design hardware and software as one experience. The lights, the touchscreen and the desktop settings all answer one question: what does the streamer need to know right now?','If I did it again, I would test hotkeys in real streams, not only design reviews, to confirm the dimmed off state stays readable under real lighting.']}
]},
{id:'1177pay',highlight:'Improved bill payment, donations and card binding in a consumer payment app, plus widgets and loading motion',coverNatural:true,projectType:'UI Design · B2C Mobile Payment',timeline:'2021 – 2023',platform:'Mobile App',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'Making bill payment quick and easy, starting from the very first payment.',labels:['UI Design','B2C Mobile Payment'],title:'1177PAY Mobile Payment App',
scope:'Bill details, donation page, card-binding flow, widget, loading animation, flowcharts, Design Guideline maintenance',collaborators:'PO · Other designers',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',items:['1177PAY is a mobile payment app. Users link credit cards, bank accounts and e-invoice barcodes to pay quickly, and get bills and payment confirmations through push notifications.','Goal: make paying bills fast, and turn first-time users of the company’s payment services into loyal app members.','Several designers worked on the app. I owned bill details, the donation page, the card-binding flow, and details like the widget and loading animation.']},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Bills and donations',text:'Reordered the information in bill details, and redesigned the main screen for browsing charities on the donation page.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Easier card binding',text:'Showed each card’s issuing bank logo, and improved the camera screen for scanning a credit card and filling in its details automatically.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Visual details',text:'A widget in light and dark mode, a loading animation made in After Effects, and the App Store screenshots.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Converting first-time users: many people meet 1177PAY through the company’s payment services rather than the app store, so the app has to show its value right away.','Complex payment flows: payments involve many checkpoints and branches, which have to be documented clearly enough for engineers to build.','Trust when adding a card: linking a credit card is a sensitive step, so users need to recognize their card and understand what the camera is doing.','Keeping a growing product consistent: as new features are added, the Design Guideline has to stay up to date so every screen still feels like the same product.']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Turning requirements into screens',text:'Working with the PO, I designed screens from the wireframes and wrote the design specifications.',imgId:'pay-screens',natural:true},
{title:'• Making card binding easier to trust',text:'Each card shows its bank’s logo so users recognize it instantly. I also made the card-scanning camera screen easier to understand and use.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Bill details and donations',text:'I reordered the information in bill details, and redesigned the main screen where users browse charities on the donation page.',noImage:true},
{title:'• Widget design',text:'Designed in both light and dark mode.',imgId:'pay-widget',natural:true},
{title:'• Loading motion design',text:'Inspired by a wallet’s snap button opening, I created the loading animation in After Effects.',imgId:'pay-loading',natural:true},
{title:'• Color palette',text:'Built on the blue of the company’s corporate identity, paired with grays and whites for a clean, trustworthy feel.',imgId:'pay-color',natural:true},
{title:'Flowcharts',text:'From the finished screens, I drew flowcharts marking checkpoints and screen transitions.',imgId:'pay-flow',natural:true},
{title:'Design handoff',text:'I delivered the designs and sliced assets through Sketch and Zeplin.',imgId:'pay-handoff',natural:true}
]},
{heading:'Results',type:'text',items:['I delivered bill details, the donation page, the card-binding flow, the widget, the loading animation, flowcharts and App Store screenshots, and kept the Design Guideline current.']},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['In a payment product, trust is built in the details: a familiar bank logo on each card, a clear state at every checkpoint, and reminders that arrive before users have to think about their bills.']},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'pay-feature-pay',natural:true},{imgId:'pay-feature-push',imgId2:'pay-feature-scan',natural:true}]}
]},
{id:'17backstage',highlight:'Redesigned the merchant back office with a new design guideline; leadership adopted it across every product line',coverNatural:true,projectType:'UI Design · B2B Back Office',timeline:'2021 – 2023',platform:'Web back office',timelineFirst:true,coverHeadline:'Turning a templated back office into a clear, consistent system for small merchants.',labels:['UI Design','B2B Back Office','Redesign'],title:'1177 Tech Merchant Back Office',
scope:'Redesign proposal, layout and interaction design, Design Guideline, wireframes, mockups and handoff',collaborators:'Company leadership · Product manager · Frontend engineers',
blocks:[
{heading:'Background',type:'text',title:'Small merchants needed a simpler way to accept payments',items:['1177 Tech provides third-party payment collection, letting merchants accept credit cards, convenience-store payments and virtual-account transfers on their own online store platforms.','The back office was an MVP for small merchants and charities that need to collect payments but don’t want to apply for a card terminal or LINE Pay. The goal: quick to sign up, intuitive to use.','The first version was built on a template, and leadership found it hard to use. As the company’s only UI designer, I led the redesign.']},
{heading:'Project at a Glance',pin:1,type:'cards',items:[
{compact:true,noImage:true,title:'Proposal adopted company-wide',text:'Leadership chose my redesign proposal and asked me to apply its design guideline to every product line in the company.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'1',statUnit:'designer, end to end',title:'The only designer, end to end',text:'From the proposal and the Design Guideline to wireframes, mockups and handoff, I designed the whole system.'},
{compact:true,noImage:true,stat:'3',statUnit:'layout improvements',title:'Three layout improvements',text:'A top menu set apart from content, detail pages shown as popups, and a collapsible step bar for long forms.'}
]},
{heading:'Problems & Challenges',type:'bullets',items:['Too much information: lots of data, and users need to find features fast.','Unclear hierarchy: a vertical menu next to vertical data lists looked like the same level.','Endless scrolling: details were listed one per line, making pages very long while the right half of the screen sat mostly empty.','Cramped forms: the step bar on the left of form pages squeezed the width of the form itself.','Easy-to-miss errors: errors in the application form were shown only as text, so users could easily overlook them.']},
{heading:'Design Decisions',type:'text-image',items:[
{title:'Leading with the reasoning: a proposal built around how people read',text:'Instead of just new screens, I presented a deck explaining why each component sits where it does: following reading order, guiding the eye, and making risky actions clear. Leadership chose it, and it became the basis of the new Design Guideline.',noImage:true},
{title:'Reorganizing the screen layout',text:'The old left-side menu looked too similar to the vertical data lists. I moved it to the top, with a shadow and a different background to separate it from the content.\n\nI considered keeping the side menu, but with so many items it would take too much space and pull focus from the content.',imgId:'bs-layout',natural:true},
{title:'Rearranging detail pages, so users no longer scroll endlessly',text:'Detail pages listed one item per line, making pages long with an empty right half. We split information into columns with wrapping rules by field length, and turned details into a popup so users can compare records easily.',imgId:'bs-detail',natural:true},
{title:'Consolidating form pages',text:'The step bar on the left squeezed the form. I made it expandable and collapsible, so users jump straight to the section they need.',imgId:'bs-form',natural:true},
{title:'• Earlier, clearer error messages',text:'Errors used to be text only. Now users are warned before moving on, with an icon, and each field shows its progress and state.',imgId:'bs-form-hints',natural:true},
{title:'A new Design Guideline',text:'From the proposal, I built a shared component library to keep systems consistent. Leadership applied it to every product line, and the visual designer used it for marketing too.',pairIds:['bs-guide-input','bs-guide-dropdown','bs-guide-icon-btn','bs-guide-font']},
{title:'Discussing wireframes',text:'I used wireframes to discuss component usage and screen layouts with the product manager.',imgId:'bs-wireframe',natural:true},
{title:'Building mockups',text:'I designed the screens in Sketch and uploaded them to Zeplin for handoff to the frontend engineers.',noImage:true}
]},
{heading:'Final Product',pin:2,type:'text-image',items:[{imgId:'bs-final-1',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-2',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-3',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-4',natural:true},{imgId:'bs-final-5',natural:true}]},
{heading:'Results',type:'text',items:['The back office was still an MVP, so the impact was internal: leadership adopted my proposal, the Design Guideline went company-wide, and the visual designer used it for marketing.']},
{heading:'Reflection',type:'text',items:['A redesign wins on its reasoning, not just its screens. Explaining layout through reading order, visual flow and risk is what convinced leadership, and turned one redesign into a company-wide guideline.']}
]}
];
